  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLatest ensemble forecast models continue to support an \nupper level trough moving through eastern New York and western \nNew England Sunday afternoon into Monday afternoon before\ndeparting the Northeast Monday night. This brings high \nconfidence for widespread rain showers Sunday afternoon through\nearly Monday evening. There are some fluctuations with the \nensemble members on the position and speed of the upper level \ntrough as it could slow down to our north. The latest forecast \nsupports for Sunday afternoon into early Monday morning to see \nrainfall totals across the southern Adirondacks, Mohawk Valley, \nand portions of the Lake George-Saratoga region between 0.5 \ninches and 1 inch. While elsewhere across eastern New York and \nwestern New England could range between a tenth to a quarter of \nan inch through Monday morning. The one key forecast element \nthat has been consistent for Sunday afternoon and evening is the\nbands of heavy rainfall stays to our west and north. However, a\nslight shift in the track could bring locally higher amounts to\nthe southern Adirondacks. Flooding concerns are low at this \nforecast time for the southern Adirondacks, Mohawk Valley, and \nLake George-Saratoga region as these locations missed the \nheaviest rainfall from this week's event and dry conditions are \nin store for today and Saturday. Typical nuisance spots and poor\ndrainage locations could experience minor flooding impacts if a\nheavy downpour occurs Sunday evening in the southern \nAdirondacks, Mohawk Valley, and Lake George-Saratoga region.\n\nAs the upper level trough slides eastward over the Northeast\nMonday, there are two scenarios that could occur for Monday \nmorning into Monday evening for across eastern New York and\nwestern New England. Scenario number one is currently supported\nby the latest forecast.\n\nScenario 1: A low pressure system at the surface moves over the\nAtlantic Coast bringing enough moisture and instability for\nperiods of moderate to heavy rainfall to the eastern Catskills,\nMid- Hudson Valley, Taconics, Berkshires, Litchfield Hills, and\nsouthern Vermont. Rainfall totals could range from 8 AM Monday \nto 8 PM Monday to 0.5 inches to 1 inch. Forecast confidence is \nlow on rainfall totals as they will be dependent on where \nmoderate rainfall does occur, as there could be locally higher \namounts. Minor flooding could occur with small rivers, creeks, \nand streams in these locations as isolated instances of flash \nflooding could occur.\n\nScenario 2: The surface low pressure system stays further south\nand east and lower rainfall totals than scenario one could \noccur for the eastern Catskills, Mid-Hudson Valley, Taconics, \nBerkshires, Litchfield Hills, and southern Vermont. \n\nLatest probabilities from the National Blend of Model data for \n24 hour precipitation totals from 8 PM Sunday to 8 PM Monday\nreaching or exceeding 1 inch are still consistent from\nyesterday's afternoon forecast ranging between 30 and 40 \npercent. For 24 hour precipitation totals reaching or exceeding \n0.5 inches is between 60 and 70 percent. Confidence is higher \nfor widespread totals to be between 0.25 inches and 0.5 inches. We\nwill continue to monitor the forecast trends and keep up to \ndate with the latest forecast as we get closer to Sunday.",
